-- The 2023 Merger Guidelines, section 4: the analytical, economic and evidentiary tools. AGENCY ENFORCEMENT POLICY, NOT
-- LAW. Only the parts of section 4 that state a computation or a test are encoded here: the metrics of 4.2.B and
-- footnote 72, the market definition tools of 4.3, the HMT and the SSNIP (4.3.A, 4.3.B, footnote 84), recapture and
-- critical loss (4.3.C), targeting (4.3.D.1), geographic shares (4.3.D.2), participants (4.4.A) and shares (4.4.B). The
-- rest of section 4 describes evidence and how the Agencies weigh it; NOTES.md lists it as inert, part by part.
--
-- An answer is NOTHING when an input is impossible (a negative quantity, a share of an empty market) or when a ratio
-- has nothing to divide by.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mg-nouns`

§ `4. Analytical, Economic, and Evidentiary Tools`

§§ `4.2.B Considerations When Terms Are Set by Firms`

-- [pdf page 38 of 51]: "The diversion ratio is the fraction of unit sales lost by the first product due to a change in
-- terms, such as an increase in its price, that would be diverted to the second product."
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 4.2.B [pdf page 38 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Diversion facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the diversion ratio of` MEANS
    IF     facts's `unit sales lost by the first product` GREATER THAN 0
       AND NOT (facts's `units diverted to the second product` LESS THAN 0)
       AND NOT (facts's `units diverted to the second product` GREATER THAN facts's `unit sales lost by the first product`)
    THEN JUST (facts's `units diverted to the second product` DIVIDED BY facts's `unit sales lost by the first product`)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- [pdf page 38 of 51]: "The diversion ratio from one of the products of one firm to a group of products made by other
-- firms, defined analogously, is sometimes referred to as the aggregate diversion ratio or the recapture rate."
-- Footnote 85 ([pdf page 45 of 51]) uses the recapture rate of 4.3.C for the same thing.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 4.2.B and footnote 85 [pdf pages 38 and 45 of 51]
GIVEN `units lost` IS A NUMBER
      `units diverted to each product of the group` IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the aggregate diversion ratio of` MEANS
    IF     `units lost` GREATER THAN 0
       AND all (GIVEN u YIELD NOT (u LESS THAN 0)) `units diverted to each product of the group`
       AND NOT (sum `units diverted to each product of the group` GREATER THAN `units lost`)
    THEN JUST (sum `units diverted to each product of the group` DIVIDED BY `units lost`)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- [pdf page 38 of 51]: the value of diverted sales "is equal to the number of units that would be diverted from the first
-- firm to the second, multiplied by the difference between the second firm's price and the incremental cost of the
-- diverted sales." A helper: it computes on whatever it is given, a negative quantity included. The reading that uses it,
-- the upward pricing pressure indicator below, refuses impossible inputs.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 4.2.B [pdf page 38 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Diversion facts`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the value of diverted sales of` MEANS
    facts's `units diverted to the second product` TIMES (facts's `the second product's price` MINUS facts's `incremental cost of the diverted sales, per unit`)

-- [pdf page 38 of 51]: "The ratio of the value of diverted sales to the revenues lost by the first firm can be an indicator
-- of the upward pricing pressure". An indicator, not a test: nothing here says how high is too high. NOTHING when no
-- revenues were lost, or when a quantity, price or cost is negative or more units are diverted than were lost; a price
-- below incremental cost is possible, and gives a negative indicator.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 4.2.B [pdf page 38 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Diversion facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the upward pricing pressure indicator of` MEANS
    IF     facts's `revenues lost by the first firm` GREATER THAN 0
       AND NOT (facts's `unit sales lost by the first product` LESS THAN 0)
       AND NOT (facts's `units diverted to the second product` LESS THAN 0)
       AND NOT (facts's `units diverted to the second product` GREATER THAN facts's `unit sales lost by the first product`)
       AND NOT (facts's `the second product's price` LESS THAN 0)
       AND NOT (facts's `incremental cost of the diverted sales, per unit` LESS THAN 0)
    THEN JUST (`the value of diverted sales of` facts DIVIDED BY facts's `revenues lost by the first firm`)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- Footnote 72 ([pdf page 37 of 51]): "The margin on incremental units is the difference between incremental revenue
-- (often equal to price) and incremental cost on those units." A helper: it computes on whatever it is given; no reading
-- in this encoding uses it.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 4.2, footnote 72 [pdf page 37 of 51]
GIVEN `incremental revenue` IS A NUMBER
      `incremental cost` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the margin on incremental units of` MEANS `incremental revenue` MINUS `incremental cost`

§§ `4.3 Market Definition`

-- [pdf pages 41 and 42 of 51]: "the Agencies may rely on any one or more of the following to identify a relevant antitrust
-- market", A to D. The caller lists the tools whose evidence identifies the candidate market; the practical indicia of C
-- are the caller's own reading ("Various practical indicia may identify a relevant market in different settings").
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 4.3, items A to D [pdf pages 41 and 42 of 51]
GIVEN `the tools that identify the market` IS A LIST OF `Market definition tool`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a relevant antitrust market is identified, by the tools of 4.3` IF
    NOT (null `the tools that identify the market`)

-- 4.3.A ([pdf pages 42 and 43 of 51]): the HMT asks whether a hypothetical monopolist "likely would undertake at least a
-- small but significant and non-transitory increase in price ("SSNIP") or other worsening of terms ("SSNIPT") for at
-- least one product in the group." Footnote 84 ([pdf page 44 of 51]): "The five percent price increase is not a threshold
-- of competitive harm from the merger." So the answer says only whether the group is a relevant market. An empty group
-- is no candidate market: NOTHING.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, sections 4.3.A and 4.3.B, footnote 84 [pdf pages 42 to 44 of 51]
GIVEN `the candidate market` IS A LIST OF `Product in the candidate market`
GIVETH A MAYBE `HMT answer`
`the HMT answer for` MEANS
    IF null `the candidate market`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (IF any (GIVEN p YIELD p's `a hypothetical monopolist likely would undertake at least a SSNIPT on it`) `the candidate market`
               THEN `the candidate market is a relevant antitrust market, by the HMT; this is not a finding of harm`
               ELSE `the HMT does not show the candidate market to be a relevant antitrust market`)

-- 4.3.B "Magnitude of the SSNIPT" ([pdf page 44 of 51]): "the Agencies will often use a SSNIP of five percent of the price
-- charged ... The Agencies, however, may consider a different term or a price increase that is larger or smaller than
-- five percent." A SSNIP is an increase, so a percentage of zero or less is NOTHING.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 4.3.B, Magnitude of the SSNIPT [pdf page 44 of 51]
GIVEN size IS A `Size of the SSNIP`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the SSNIP percentage of` MEANS
    CONSIDER size
    WHEN `five percent, as the Agencies often use` THEN 5
    WHEN `another percentage the Agencies consider` pct THEN pct

@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 4.3.B, Magnitude of the SSNIPT [pdf page 44 of 51]
GIVEN size IS A `Size of the SSNIP`
      price IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the SSNIP on a price of` MEANS
    IF price LESS THAN 0 OR NOT (`the SSNIP percentage of` size GREATER THAN 0)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`the SSNIP percentage of` size DIVIDED BY 100 TIMES price)

-- [pdf page 44 of 51]: "the Agencies may derive an implicit price for the service of transporting oil over a pipeline as
-- the difference between the price the pipeline firm paid for oil at one end and the price it sold the oil for at the
-- other and base the SSNIP on this implicit price."
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 4.3.B [pdf page 44 of 51]
GIVEN `the price paid at one end` IS A NUMBER
      `the price sold for at the other` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the implicit price of the pipeline's service` MEANS `the price sold for at the other` MINUS `the price paid at one end`

§§ `4.3.C Evidence and Tools for Carrying Out the Hypothetical Monopolist Test`

-- [pdf page 44 of 51]: "A price increase is profitable when the recapture rate is high enough that the incremental profits
-- from the increased price plus the incremental profits from the recaptured sales going to other products in the
-- candidate market exceed the profits lost when sales are diverted outside the candidate market."
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 4.3.C [pdf page 44 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Recapture facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the price increase is profitable, by recapture` IF
        facts's `incremental profits from the increased price`
         PLUS facts's `incremental profits from the sales recaptured by other products in the candidate market`
    GREATER THAN facts's `profits lost on sales diverted outside the candidate market`

-- [pdf pages 44 and 45 of 51]: "The worsening of terms raises the hypothetical monopolist's profits if the predicted loss is
-- less than the critical loss."
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 4.3.C [pdf pages 44 and 45 of 51]
GIVEN `predicted loss` IS A NUMBER
      `critical loss` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the worsening raises the hypothetical monopolist's profits, by critical loss` IF
    `predicted loss` LESS THAN `critical loss`

-- [pdf page 44 of 51]: "The "critical loss" is defined as the number of lost unit sales that would leave profits
-- unchanged." The text gives no formula. With a constant incremental cost per unit (fork TL-1), profits Q(P - C) before
-- and (Q - L)(P(1 + s) - C) after are equal at L = Q s P / (P(1 + s) - C). NOTHING when an input is impossible, or when
-- the incremental cost exceeds the price before: the margin is then negative, and the formula's L exceeds Q, so no loss
-- of up to all the sales leaves profits unchanged. At C = P, L = Q.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 4.3.C [pdf pages 44 and 45 of 51]
GIVEN facts IS A `Breakeven facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the critical loss, in units, of` MEANS
    IF     q LESS THAN 0 OR (NOT (p GREATER THAN 0)) OR c LESS THAN 0 OR (NOT (s GREATER THAN 0))
        OR c GREATER THAN p
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (q TIMES s TIMES p DIVIDED BY (p TIMES (1 PLUS s) MINUS c))
    WHERE
        q MEANS facts's `unit sales before the worsening of terms`
        p MEANS facts's `the price before`
        c MEANS facts's `incremental cost per unit`
        s MEANS facts's `the price increase, as a fraction of the price`

§§ `4.3.D.1 Targeted Trading Partners`

-- [pdf page 45 of 51]: "For targeting to be feasible, two conditions typically must be met. First, the suppliers engaging
-- in targeting must be able to set different terms for targeted customers than other customers. ... Second, the targeted
-- customers must not be likely to defeat a targeted worsening of terms by arbitrage". Then the Agencies "may identify
-- relevant markets defined around those targeted customers".
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 4.3.D.1 [pdf page 45 of 51]
GIVEN `the suppliers can set different terms for targeted customers` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the targeted customers are likely to defeat a targeted worsening of terms by arbitrage` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `targeting is feasible, so a market around the targeted customers may be identified` IF
        `the suppliers can set different terms for targeted customers`
    AND NOT `the targeted customers are likely to defeat a targeted worsening of terms by arbitrage`

§§ `4.3.D.2 Geographic Markets`

-- 4.3.D.2.a ([pdf page 46 of 51]): "When calculating market shares, sales made from supplier locations in the geographic
-- market are included, regardless of whether the customer making the purchase travelled from outside". 4.3.D.2.b ([pdf
-- page 47 of 51]): "Some suppliers may be located outside the boundaries of the geographic market, but their sales to
-- customers located within the market are included when calculating market shares".
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, sections 4.3.D.2.a and 4.3.D.2.b [pdf pages 46 and 47 of 51]
GIVEN kind IS A `Kind of geographic market`
      sale IS A `Sale`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the sale is counted in the geographic market` MEANS
    CONSIDER kind
    WHEN `based on the locations of suppliers` THEN sale's `made from a supplier location in the region`
    WHEN `based on the locations of customers` THEN sale's `made to a customer location in the region`

@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, sections 4.3.D.2.a, 4.3.D.2.b and 4.4 [pdf pages 46, 47 and 51 of 51]
GIVEN kind IS A `Kind of geographic market`
      sales IS A LIST OF `Sale`
      seller IS A STRING
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the seller's share of the geographic market, in percent` MEANS
    IF any (GIVEN x YIELD x's `the amount of the sale` LESS THAN 0) sales OR NOT (total GREATER THAN 0)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (100 TIMES `the seller's amount` DIVIDED BY total)
    WHERE
        counted MEANS filter (GIVEN x YIELD `the sale is counted in the geographic market` kind x) sales
        total MEANS sum (map (GIVEN x YIELD x's `the amount of the sale`) counted)
        `the seller's amount` MEANS sum (map (GIVEN x YIELD x's `the amount of the sale`) (filter (GIVEN x YIELD x's `the seller` EQUALS seller) counted))

§§ `4.4.A Market Participants`

-- [pdf pages 50 and 51 of 51], in the text's order: current suppliers; vertically integrated firms "to the extent that their
-- inclusion accurately reflects their competitive significance"; firms "committed to entering the market in the near
-- future"; and "rapid entrants", which "very likely would rapidly enter with direct competitive impact in the event of a
-- small but significant change in competitive conditions, without incurring significant sunk costs". Other entry "is
-- considered in Section 3.2". And "a firm's possession of idle or swing capacity alone does not make that firm a rapid
-- entrant": the capacity is not one of the conditions.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 4.4.A [pdf pages 50 and 51 of 51]
GIVEN firm IS A `Firm considered as a participant`
GIVETH A `Market participation`
`the firm's participation in the relevant market` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF firm's `the firm currently supplies products in the relevant market` THEN `a participant: it currently supplies the relevant market`
        IF firm's `the firm is vertically integrated, and including it accurately reflects its competitive significance` THEN `a participant: a vertically integrated firm`
        IF firm's `the firm has committed to entering the market in the near future` THEN `a participant: it has committed to entering in the near future`
        IF     firm's `the firm very likely would rapidly enter with direct competitive impact on a small but significant change in conditions`
           AND NOT (firm's `the firm would incur significant sunk costs to enter`)
            THEN `a participant: a rapid entrant`
        OTHERWISE `not a participant: its entry, if any, is considered under Section 3.2`

§§ `4.4.B Market Shares`

-- [pdf page 51 of 51]: "The Agencies measure each firm's market share using metrics that are informative about the market
-- realities of competition". The text lists metrics (revenues, unit sales, revenues from recently acquired customers,
-- capacities or reserves, non-price indicators) and says "multiple metrics may be informative"; whichever is used, a
-- firm's share is its amount over the market's total, in percent. The result is the input Guideline 1 reads.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 4.4.B [pdf page 51 of 51]
GIVEN amounts IS A LIST OF `Firm's measured amount`
GIVETH A MAYBE (LIST OF `Firm in the relevant market`)
`the market shares on the measure` MEANS
    IF any (GIVEN a YIELD a's `its amount on the measure` LESS THAN 0) amounts OR NOT (total GREATER THAN 0)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (map (GIVEN a YIELD `Firm in the relevant market` WITH
                        `the firm's name` IS a's `the measured firm`
                        `the firm's pre-merger share` IS 100 TIMES a's `its amount on the measure` DIVIDED BY total
                        `the firm is a party to the merger` IS a's `the measured firm is a party to the merger`)
                   amounts)
    WHERE
        total MEANS sum (map (GIVEN a YIELD a's `its amount on the measure`) amounts)
